{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father's assassination. As he was still a minor his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. \n\nIn 1160 Raymond was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi during a raid on the county and was held prisoner for nearly a decade. *Imprisonment and Release* During his imprisonment his step cousin Bohemond III of Antioch acted as regent of Tripoli. Raymond was released in 1173 after a ransom was paid and he returned to Tripoli to take control of the county. \n\nUpon his return Raymond became involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. *Regency and Later Life* Raymond was a cousin of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and was appointed as regent for the king during his periods of illness. He was a strong advocate for a peaceful resolution to the conflict with Saladin and opposed the aggressive policies of Raynald of Châtillon. \n\nRaymond's regency was marked by his efforts to strengthen the kingdom's defenses and to negotiate a peace treaty with Saladin. However his efforts were ultimately unsuccessful and the kingdom was defeated at the Battle of Hattin. After the battle Raymond fled to Tyre where he died in 1187 possibly due to illness or old age. His death marked the end of an era in the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem as the crusader states began to crumble under the pressure of Muslim attacks. Raymond was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob":